SLIDING WINDOW MEDIAN

(Hard · FAANG-level · Data Structures)

This problem instantly levels up your repo because it mixes heaps + sliding window logic.

1️⃣ Problem Statement

You are given:

An integer array nums

An integer k (window size)

For each sliding window of size k, return the median of the elements inside the window.

Example
Input:
nums = [1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7]
k = 3

Output:
[1, -1, -1, 3, 5, 6]

2️⃣ Why This Problem Is Important

✔ Combines priority queues (heaps)
✔ Very common Google / Amazon problem
✔ Tests real-world DS usage
✔ Hard to solve cleanly ❗

3️⃣ Key Idea (Intuition)

Maintain two heaps:

Heap	Purpose
Max Heap	Stores smaller half
Min Heap	Stores larger half

So that:

Size difference ≤ 1

Median is:

Top of max heap (odd k)

Average of both tops (even k)

4️⃣ Algorithm (High-Level)

Add new element to one of the heaps

Rebalance heaps

Remove outgoing element (sliding window)

Rebalance again

Compute median

5️⃣ Java Code (Interview-Ready)
import java.util.*;

class SlidingWindowMedian {

    PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =
        new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

    PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap =
        new PriorityQueue<>();

    private void balance() {
        if (maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size() + 1)
            minHeap.add(maxHeap.poll());
        else if (minHeap.size() > maxHeap.size())
            maxHeap.add(minHeap.poll());
    }

    private void addNum(int num) {
        if (maxHeap.isEmpty() || num <= maxHeap.peek())
            maxHeap.add(num);
        else
            minHeap.add(num);
        balance();
    }

    private void removeNum(int num) {
        if (num <= maxHeap.peek())
            maxHeap.remove(num);
        else
            minHeap.remove(num);
        balance();
    }

    public double[] medianSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {

        double[] result = new double[nums.length - k + 1];
        int index = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            addNum(nums[i]);

            if (i >= k - 1) {
                if (k % 2 == 1)
                    result[index++] = maxHeap.peek();
                else
                    result[index++] =
                        ((double) maxHeap.peek()
                        + minHeap.peek()) / 2.0;

                removeNum(nums[i - k + 1]);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}

6️⃣ Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 Why Two Heaps?

Lower half → MaxHeap

Higher half → MinHeap

🔹 Balancing
if (maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size() + 1)


Keeps size difference ≤ 1

🔹 Removing Sliding Window Element
maxHeap.remove(num);


Java heap supports removal by value

Cost: O(n) (acceptable in interviews)

